{
  // THE WHOLE PROJECT. `npm run typecheck` runs this, then tsconfig.froggie.json
  // re-runs the vendored game at its own stricter bar.
  //
  // This used to be `include: ["src/froggie"]` and nothing else — meaning every
  // .jsx file in the portfolio (the router, all five worlds, the project pages)
  // was checked by absolutely nothing. That is not a theoretical gap: two
  // crashes shipped straight through it. A missing import
  // (`ReferenceError: PROJECTS is not defined`) and a property read off a shape
  // that lacked it (`shot.src` on a sliced-strip shot, which has no `src`) both
  // passed `typecheck` AND `build`, then white-screened the design world in the
  // browser. `vite build` only transpiles, and esbuild treats an unknown
  // identifier as a global rather than an error, so neither tool could have
  // caught them. Both are caught here now — see the regression note below.
  //
  // `strict` is OFF deliberately. The job is to catch the mistakes that actually
  // happen in this codebase — a name that doesn't exist, a property that isn't
  // on the object, a required prop left off a component — not to migrate plain
  // JSX to TypeScript. Turning strict on adds hundreds of nullability
  // complaints on untyped JSX, and a check with permanent known errors is one
  // nobody reads, which is how this file ended up covering nothing in the first
  // place. It runs at 0 errors today and should be kept there.
  //
  // Two files opt out with `@ts-nocheck`, each explaining itself in place:
  // Lanyard.jsx (react-three-fiber intrinsics, some invented at runtime by
  // `extend()`) and DomeGallery.jsx (vendored verbatim). They are not listed in
  // `exclude` because that would do nothing — `exclude` only filters the
  // `include` glob, and both are reached as imports regardless.
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "jsx": "react-jsx",
    "noEmit": true,
    "allowJs": true,
    "checkJs": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "strict": false,
    "noImplicitAny": false
  },
  "include": ["src"]
}
